      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n# Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nNearly every product organization produces a roadmap, yet remarkably few products follow one to success. The failure is rarely a matter of poor planning discipline. It is structural: the conventional roadmap rests on assumptions that guarantee its own obsolescence.\n\nThe first and deepest flaw is that roadmaps describe outputs when products succeed on outcomes. A typical roadmap is a list of features attached to dates. But features are solutions, and solutions are hypotheses about what customers need. By committing to a solution a year in advance, a team forecloses the learning that would produce a better one. The roadmap answers \"what will we build?\" while never asking \"will it work?\" Teams that ship the roadmap faithfully can still ship products nobody wants — they executed the plan and ignored the evidence.\n\nThe second flaw is false precision. Software development involves irreducible uncertainty; attaching dates to uncertain work converts a plan into a promise. Once the roadmap becomes an accountability instrument, behavior distorts: estimates get padded, scope gets cut to hit deadlines, and quality erodes. Or the dates are missed and trust erodes instead. Either way, the roadmap fails socially before it fails technically.\n\nThird, roadmaps are static artifacts in dynamic environments. Markets shift, competitors move, user research confounds expectations. A roadmap drafted in an annual planning cycle and rarely revisited is a snapshot mistaken for a moving picture. When reality diverges from the document, the roadmap is quietly abandoned rather than revised, and with it goes whatever strategic coherence it carried.\n\nFourth, many roadmaps are political documents first and strategic ones second. They serve as negotiation instruments: a slot for the loudest salesperson's client, a line item for the executive with the strongest opinion, a gesture to appease an anxious board. Items accrete without argument about trade-offs. The result is not a strategy expressed as a sequence but a queue of promises — and a queue is not a direction.\n\nUnderlying all of this is a deeper misunderstanding: the belief that the plan is the product of planning. The value of planning lies in the thinking it forces — what we believe, why we believe it, what evidence would change our minds. The document is merely the residue. Organizations that treat the residue as sacred lose the agility the thinking was meant to provide.\n\nRoadmaps that work look different. They are anchored in outcomes — problems to solve, metrics to move — rather than feature lists. They express direction and confidence rather than precision: what we are doing now, what is likely next, what might come later. They are living documents, revised as learning accumulates rather than defended as commitments. And every item traces back to an articulated strategy, so that \"no\" is as visible as \"yes.\"\n\nMost roadmaps fail not because teams plan, but because they plan the wrong things: features instead of outcomes, dates instead of direction, documents instead of decisions. The remedy is not better forecasting but honesty about uncertainty — a roadmap built to change its mind.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nA product roadmap is meant to align teams around a future state, but most roadmaps fail for a simple reason: they are treated as promises rather than plans under uncertainty.\n\nWhen a roadmap is built backwards from deadlines, it reflects what leadership hopes to announce more than what the product team can credibly achieve. Teams estimate work using incomplete information, then commit to dates without room for discovery, dependencies, or quality. The first sign of trouble triggers a choice between lowering quality, changing scope, or missing the date. Most choose the path that causes the least visible pain: they miss the date and damage trust.\n\nAnother failure mode is that roadmaps are often outputs-based instead of outcomes-based. Listing features—“login, dashboard, notifications”—may look like strategy, but features are hypotheses, not results. If a roadmap does not define the problem it is solving or the metric that signals success, teams can ship everything on schedule and still deliver no impact. Product management becomes order-taking, and engineering becomes execution for its own sake.\n\nPolitical roadmapping is also common. Many roadmaps are assembled to win budget, impress executives, or coordinate across functions, not to guide real product work. That makes them brittle. When new information arrives—customer feedback, market shifts, a competitor move, or a technical constraint—the roadmap cannot change gracefully because its dates have already been used for forecasts, contracts, or promotions. Rather than updating the plan, teams defend it, and the organization learns to distrust roadmaps as marketing artifacts.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps fail because they are static. In a healthy product organization, the roadmap is an evolving set of problems, bets, and sequencing decisions. It should include uncertainty, assumptions, and experiments. Too many teams publish a polished slide deck and then stop revisiting it. As a result, the roadmap is either ignored or treated as obsolete the moment reality intrudes.\n\nA better roadmap is shorter, clearer, and more honest. It prioritizes problems and outcomes, shows ranges rather than fixed dates when appropriate, and leaves space for learning. It is not a commitment to a feature list; it is a shared theory of how the product will create value. When organizations treat roadmaps as adaptive instruments rather than declarations of certainty, they become far less likely to fail.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
